# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# .dockerignore — the canonical "what doesn't ship to production" list.
#
# This file is read by `docker build` to determine the build context. Paths
# listed here are NOT copied into the image. That makes it the single
# GUI-editable source of truth for excluding files from the deploy build.
#
# Two layers of exclusions live here:
#
#   1. UNIVERSAL — always excluded from any production build (this list is
#      identical between `main` and `deploy`):
#        • .git, node_modules
#        • .attic/ (archived dormant code)
#        • Payload runtime upload dirs (mounted as volumes on the VPS)
#        • Large 3D static assets (rsync'd to /opt/thehideaway/assets/ once)
#
#   2. DEPLOY-ONLY — excluded from the production build on the `deploy`
#      branch but kept on `main` for dev. Add files/dirs here when you want
#      them present during development but not in the shipped image:
#        • reference/ (foreign code kept for study)
#        • Internal feature dev notes you don't want public
#        • src/app/(frontend)/(dev)/ if you decide to gate dev-only routes
#        • Individual not-prod-ready files under src/ or public/
#
# When syncing `main → deploy` via scripts/sync-from-main.sh, the only
# expected merge conflict is in this file (and only if the deploy-only
# section was edited on both branches). Resolve by taking the deploy
# side's exclusion list as authoritative.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Universal exclusions ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.git
node_modules/
.attic/
reference/

# Secret files. The workflow materializes .env.production INTO the build
# context for `pnpm build` (to bake NEXT_PUBLIC_*), but the file itself must
# never land in any image layer. No Dockerfile currently does `COPY . .`, so
# this is defense in depth — keeps a stray future COPY from leaking all secrets.
.env
.env.*
!.env.*.example
/src/lib/auth/auth-payload/
!/src/payload/collections/Users/c/dd.js

/src/app/(frontend)/(site)/(content)/essays/DemoOfArchive.tsx

# Payload runtime upload directories — managed via Docker named volumes.
# These are written by Payload CMS at runtime (media uploads, user avatars,
# audio tracks). They must NOT be baked into the image:
#   1. Dev content would be shipped to production.
#   2. Files uploaded in production would vanish on container restart
#      unless mounted as a persistent volume.
# compose.prod.yaml mounts these as named volumes on the VPS.
public/media/
public/track_media/
public/users/
# assets/media is the Assets collection upload dir (small, same principle)
public/assets/media/

# ── Large 3D static assets (~432 MB combined) ────────────────────────────────
# These binary/texture files are committed to git for local dev but must NOT
# be baked into the production Docker image — they're served as plain static
# files and have no build-time role.
#
# compose.prod.yaml mounts them from the VPS filesystem at runtime.
#
# BOOTSTRAP (run once before first prod deploy):
#   rsync -av --progress ./public/assets/plate/     <vps>:/opt/thehideaway/assets/plate/
#   rsync -av --progress ./public/assets/rp_plaque/ <vps>:/opt/thehideaway/assets/rp_plaque/
#   rsync -av --progress ./public/assets/three/     <vps>:/opt/thehideaway/assets/three/
#
# After that, deploys are fast — the VPS host dirs persist across container
# restarts because bind mounts are never rebuilt like named volumes.
public/assets/plate/
public/assets/rp_plaque/
public/assets/three/

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ── Deploy-only exclusions (edit freely on the `deploy` branch) ─────────────
# Add files/dirs below this line that you want to keep in dev but exclude
# from the production image. Examples:
#
#   readmes/HIGHLIGHTR.md
#   readmes/NOTIFICATIONS.md
#   readmes/tour-flow/
#   src/app/(frontend)/(dev)/
#   public/assets/textures/photographj/
#
# Leaving this section empty on `main` keeps the merge into `deploy` clean.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
